BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:“Therefore\, What?” Report: Proposal [REL C 333-033]
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260919
DTSTAMP:20260912T060000Z
UID:event-assignment-1441152
SEQUENCE:0
CLASS:PUBLIC
DESCRIPTION:[] (https://byu.instructure.com/courses/35044/files/13505106/p
 review?hidden=1)\n\nEstimated time: 15 minutes\n\nThis course gives you th
 e opportunity to study the lives and teachings of living prophets. However
 \, this learning is ultimately of little value unless it leads you to act 
 in a way that brings you closer to Christ. Elder Jeffrey R. Holland said:
 \n\nPresident Boyd K. Packer\, himself a master teacher and long-time admi
 nistrator in the Church Educational System\, has a question he often asks 
 when we have made a presentation or given some sort of exhortation to one 
 another in the Twelve. He looks up as if to say\, “Are you through?” A
 nd then says to the speaker (and\, by implication\, to the rest of the gro
 up)\, “Therefore\, what?”\n\n“Therefore\, what?” I think that is w
 hat the Savior answered day in and day out as an inseparable element of Hi
 s teaching and preaching. I’ve tried to suggest that. These sermons and 
 exhortations were to no avail if the actual lives of His disciples did not
  change.[1] (#end)\n\nAs part of this course\, you will participate in an 
 experience of your own design in which you apply the teachings of Presiden
 t Oaks. You will create a proposal now based on something you have felt in
 spired to do as you studied President Oak’s talks and life\, and at the 
 end of the semester\, you will report how this experience has affected you
 . Your final report will be approximately one page and is due on the date 
 indicated in the syllabus.\n\nFor the current assignment\, you should deci
 de on and propose the experience you want to do. There are many possibilit
 ies\, but focus on something meaningful to you personally\, originating fr
 om your assigned study of President Oaks’s messages and ministry. Your p
 roject should be longitudinal in nature—something to work on over the co
 urse of the semester\, starting this week or next\, up until near the dead
 line\, rather than a one-time or short-term assignment. For example:\n\n* 
 You may want to respond to a specific challenge he gave and create an acti
 on plan for the next months.\n\n* You may study a number of additional add
 resses by President Oaks using the strategies we’ve learned.\n\n* You ma
 y choose to study a collection of talks President Oaks has given on a part
 icular topic\, or talks/devotionals/broadcasts/articles he has specificall
 y directed to youth and young adults.\n\n* You may prayerfully choose a pr
 inciple from one of the talks you read by President Oaks and decide how to
  apply this principle in your life over the next months by taking a specif
 ic action.\n\nFor your proposal assignment\, in about 6–8 sentences tota
 l\, articulate the following:\n\n* Exactly what you plan to do and what in
 spired you as you studied Pres. Oaks's teachings.\n\n* How you will measur
 e it --for how many weeks\, how often\, how much content\, and/or how many
  hours you’ll spend\, recognizing that it should last over most of the s
 emester. Don't propose something wimpy that doesn't stretch you at all\, b
 ut you don't have to impress me or prove anything to anyone (including you
 rself). You are busy\, you are juggling lots of different responsibilities
 \, and you don't have to be superhuman. What would honestly bless your lif
 e?  \n\n* Why you believe this plan will benefit you. Make sure you spen
 d some time thinking about how to balance a meaningful project with an ong
 oing scope that is manageable\; don’t hesitate to be prayerful about it.
  I'm serious\; pray about it\, and trust your promptings. \n\nNote: Come 
 back and check on any instructor comments once this is graded. You may fin
 d that your proposal needs some tweaking to be an approved project. Once a
 pproved\, you should begin working on your goals and keep some kind of rec
 ord or notes about your experience so that you can report on it accurately
  at the end of the semester. You will not be required to submit your notes
 \, but you will need to submit a brief and thorough report on how it went.
  A significant portion of your score will be based on how completely you c
 arry out your proposed project.\n\nTo submit your report:\n\n* Click on Su
 bmit Assignment at the top. A text box will appear below.\n\n* Type your p
 roposal. \n\n* Click Submit Assignment at the bottom of the text box.\n\n
 Rubric: 5 points = 1 point for describing what you’ll do\; 1 point for c
 lear\, measurable standards of how often\, how much\, and how long you'll 
 work on it\; 1 point for potential benefit\; 1 point for evidence of thoro
 ugh planning of an engaging project\; 1 point for length/submission requir
 ements.\n\nModule Item Completion (built in browser\, hidden in app)\n\n1
 \n\nReferences\n\n1 Jeffrey R. Holland\, “[Therefore\, What?] (https://
 www.churchofjesuschrist.org/study/manual/teaching-seminary-preservice-read
 ings-religion-370-471-and-475/therefore-what?lang=eng)\,” Teaching Semin
 ary: Preservice Readings
URL;VALUE=URI:https://byu.instructure.com/calendar?include_contexts=course
 _35044&month=09&year=2026#assignment_1441152
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<link rel="stylesheet" href="https://instruct
 ure-uploads.s3.amazonaws.com/account_74070000000000001/attachments/8409020
 /dp_app.css"><link rel="stylesheet" href="https://instructure-uploads.s3.a
 mazonaws.com/account_74070000000000001/attachments/8073295/canvas-byuonlin
 e-app.css"><p><img src="https://byu.instructure.com/courses/35044/files/13
 505106/preview?hidden=1" alt="" width="2389" height="554" data-decorative=
 "true" data-api-endpoint="https://byu.instructure.com/api/v1/courses/35044
 /files/13505106" data-api-returntype="File" loading="lazy"></p>\n<p><em><s
 pan style="font-size: 10pt\;">Estimated time: 15 minutes</span></em></p>\n
 <p>This course gives you the opportunity to study the lives and teachings 
 of living prophets. However\, this learning is ultimately of little value 
 unless it leads you to act in a way that brings you closer to Christ. Elde
 r Jeffrey R. Holland said:</p>\n<p style="padding-left: 40px\;">President 
 Boyd K. Packer\, himself a master teacher and long-time administrator in t
 he Church Educational System\, has a question he often asks when we have m
 ade a presentation or given some sort of exhortation to one another in the
  Twelve. He looks up as if to say\, “Are you through?” And then says t
 o the speaker (and\, by implication\, to the rest of the group)\, “There
 fore\, what?”</p>\n<p style="padding-left: 40px\;">“Therefore\, what?
 ” I think that is what the Savior answered day in and day out as an inse
 parable element of His teaching and preaching. I’ve tried to suggest tha
 t. These sermons and exhortations were to no avail if the actual lives of 
 His disciples did not change.<sup><a href="#end">1</a></sup></p>\n<p>As pa
 rt of this course\, you will participate in an experience of your own desi
 gn in which you apply the teachings of President Oaks. You will create a p
 roposal now based on something you have felt inspired to do as you studied
  President Oak’s talks and life\, and at the end of the semester\, you w
 ill report how this experience has affected you. Your final report will be
  approximately one page and is due on the date indicated in the syllabus.<
 /p>\n<p>For the current assignment\, you should decide on and propose the 
 experience you want to do. There are <em>many</em> possibilities\, but foc
 us on something meaningful to you personally\, originating from your assig
 ned study of President Oaks’s messages and ministry. Your project should
  be longitudinal in nature—something to work on <strong>over the course 
 of the semester\, starting this week or next\, up until near the deadline<
 /strong>\, rather than a one-time or short-term assignment. For example:</
 p>\n<ul>\n<li>You may want to respond to a specific challenge he gave and 
 create an action plan for the next months.</li>\n<li>You may study a numbe
 r of additional addresses by President Oaks using the strategies we’ve l
 earned.</li>\n<li>You may choose to study a collection of talks President 
 Oaks has given on a particular topic\, or talks/devotionals/broadcasts/art
 icles he has specifically directed to youth and young adults.</li>\n<li>Yo
 u may prayerfully choose a principle from one of the talks you read by Pre
 sident Oaks and decide how to apply this principle in your life over the n
 ext months by taking a specific action.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr>\n<p>For your pro
 posal assignment\, in about 6–8 sentences total\, articulate the followi
 ng:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Exactly <strong>what </strong>you plan to do and what i
 nspired you as you studied Pres. Oaks's teachings.</li>\n<li>\n<strong>How
 </strong> you will measure it --for how many weeks\, <strong>how often\, h
 ow much </strong>content\, and/or how many hours you’ll spend\, recogniz
 ing that it should last over most of the semester. <em>Don't propose somet
 hing wimpy that doesn't stretch you at all\, but you don't have to impress
  me or prove anything to anyone (including yourself). You are busy\, you a
 re juggling lots of different responsibilities\, and you don't have to be 
 superhuman. What would honestly bless your life?&nbsp\;&nbsp\;</em>\n</li>
 \n<li>\n<strong>Why </strong>you believe this plan will benefit you. Make 
 sure you spend some time thinking about how to balance a meaningful projec
 t with an ongoing scope that is manageable\; don’t hesitate to be prayer
 ful about it. <em>I'm serious\; pray about it\, and trust your promptings.
 &nbsp\;</em>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<hr>\n<p><span style="background-color: #ccffc
 c\;"><strong><br>Note:</strong> Come back and check on any instructor comm
 ents once this is graded. You may find that your proposal needs some tweak
 ing to be an approved project. Once approved\, you should begin working on
  your goals and keep some kind of record or notes about your experience so
  that you can report on it accurately at the end of the semester. You will
  not be required to submit your notes\, but you will need to submit a brie
 f and thorough report on how it went. A significant portion of your score 
 will be based on how completely you carry out your proposed project.</span
 ></p>\n<p>To submit your report:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Click on Submit Assignment
  at the top. A text box will appear below.</li>\n<li>Type your proposal.&n
 bsp\;</li>\n<li>Click Submit Assignment at the bottom of the text box.</li
 >\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Rubric:</strong> 5 points = 1 point for describing wh
 at you’ll do\; 1 point for clear\, measurable standards of how often\, h
 ow much\, and how long you'll work on it\; 1 point for potential benefit\;
  1 point for evidence of thorough planning of an engaging project\; 1 poin
 t for length/submission requirements.</p>\n<p class="kl_module_progress_co
 mpletion" style="display: none\;">Module Item Completion (built in browser
 \, hidden in app)</p>\n<hr>\n<p id="expand" class="content-box" style="dis
 play: none\;"><span style="font-size: 10pt\;"><sup>1</sup> </span></p>\n<d
 iv class="kl_panels_wrapper kl_panels_expander kl_no_expander_controls">\n
 <p id="kl_panel_0" class="kl_panel_heading kl_panel_0"><span style="font-s
 ize: 18pt\;">References</span></p>\n<div id="kl_panel_0_content" class="kl
 _panel_content kl_panel_0">\n<p><span style="font-size: 10pt\;"><sup>1</su
 p> Jeffrey R. Holland\,&nbsp\;“<a href="https://www.churchofjesuschrist.
 org/study/manual/teaching-seminary-preservice-readings-religion-370-471-an
 d-475/therefore-what?lang=eng">Therefore\, What?</a>\,” <em>Teaching Sem
 inary: Preservice Readings</em></span></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<hr id="end" s
 tyle="height: 4px\; background-color: #002e5d\; color: #002e5d\;"><script 
